#My First DQN Failed

By this point, I felt ready to finally introduce Reinforcement Learning.

I had a working game environment.

I had multiple heuristic agents.

I had benchmarks.

I had a rough theoretical reference for efficient play.

Most importantly, I knew what good behaviour looked like.

A strong agent should progressively reduce uncertainty, choose useful questions, and ideally identify a character in something close to the log_2_(N) range of decisions.

So the next idea seemed straightforward:

> Give a Deep Q-Network all the possible actions and let it learn how to play.

This turned out to be much harder than I expected.

---

# The First RL Design

I chose a Deep Q-Network (DQN) using Stable-Baselines.

The initial action space combined two types of decisions:

1. **Question actions**
2. **Direct character guesses**

Conceptually, the agent could choose from:

Action Space

├── Is Sex = male?
├── Is Sex = female?
├── Is Region = Europe?
├── Is Region = Asia?
├── Is Hair color = blonde?
├── Is Industry = politics?
│
├── Guess Character 1
├── Guess Character 2
├── Guess Character 3
│
└── ...

My reasoning was simple.

These were all decisions available to a player.

A human can either:

ask another question;
or decide they know enough and make a guess.

So I initially represented both as actions in one unified discrete action space.

I expected the agent to gradually learn:

Asking good questions early is useful. Guessing becomes useful once the search space is small enough.

That was the theory.

The first training runs told a very different story.

The Action Space Was Not Balanced

The problem became obvious once I looked more carefully at the environment.

The search space contained roughly 4,200 characters.

That meant there were thousands of possible direct guess actions.

The number of question actions was tiny by comparison.

The resulting action space looked approximately like this:

Total actions: ~4,200+

Question actions: very small fraction
Guess actions: ~99.3%

So during early exploration, the overwhelming majority of randomly selected actions were direct guesses.

And almost every random guess was wrong.

The agent's early experience became something like:

Start episode
     ↓
Explore random action
     ↓
Almost certainly select a character guess
     ↓
Almost certainly guess incorrectly
     ↓
Receive negative reward
     ↓
Episode ends or trajectory becomes poor
     ↓
Repeat

The agent barely experienced the behaviour I actually wanted it to learn.

It was technically capable of asking questions.

But statistically, random exploration almost never reached them.

The Result: 0% Win Rate

The first DQN did not slowly improve.

It did not discover a weak questioning strategy.

It did not eventually approach the heuristic baselines.

It collapsed into a terrible policy.

The win rate reached:

0%

This was one of the most useful failures in the project.

My first reaction was naturally to look at the model.

Was DQN the wrong algorithm?

Was the reward too weak?

Did I need more training?

But the more I looked at the problem, the less I believed the failure was primarily about DQN itself.

The environment had given the agent an extremely unbalanced exploration problem.

I had effectively created an action space where:

Almost every possible exploratory action was immediately bad.

That changed how I thought about the phrase "let the agent explore."

Exploration is not magic.

If useful actions are extremely rare and destructive actions dominate the action space, random exploration may spend most of its time generating useless experience.

The agent cannot learn a good questioning strategy if it almost never gets the opportunity to practice questioning.

My First Important RL Environment Lesson

This was the first major point where building the game and building an RL environment started feeling like two different problems.

From a game-design perspective, the unified action space made sense.

A player really can either:

Ask a question

or:

Make a guess

But from an RL perspective, those actions had dramatically different properties.

There were:

relatively few question actions;
thousands of guess actions;
almost no chance of randomly selecting the correct guess early in an episode.

The environment was logically valid.

But logically valid did not mean easily learnable.

That distinction became one of the biggest lessons of the project.

The way a decision problem is represented can determine whether an RL agent has any realistic chance of learning it.

Trying to Control Invalid Actions

Once I understood the action imbalance, I needed to stop the agent from wasting most of its training on premature guesses.

My first instinct was action masking.

The logic was straightforward:

If remaining candidates > 1:
    allow question actions
    block direct guesses

If remaining candidates == 1:
    allow final guess

This would force the agent to participate in the actual deduction process.

Instead of immediately selecting from thousands of character guesses, it would have to reduce the search space first.

Conceptually:

Remaining candidates > 1
          │
          ▼
    Ask a question
          │
          ▼
 Update search space
          │
          ▼
Remaining candidates = 1
          │
          ▼
      Make guess

This was a much better description of the behaviour I wanted to train.

But implementing that constraint introduced another problem.

The action_mask Crash

My first attempt was to include a dynamic action_mask directly inside the Gymnasium observation dictionary.

The idea was to give the agent something like:

Observation

├── asked_mask
├── remaining_ratio
├── split_ratios
└── action_mask

The action_mask would indicate which actions were currently valid.

For example:

Question 1 → valid
Question 2 → valid
Question 3 → invalid
Guess 1     → invalid
Guess 2     → invalid
...

Then, once the search space became sufficiently small, the valid guess actions could change dynamically.

Conceptually, this seemed clean.

The environment would simply tell the agent:

Here is the current state, and here are the actions you are allowed to take.

But standard Stable-Baselines3 policies were not expecting this additional observation component in the way I had implemented it.

The environment crashed because the observation structure no longer matched what the policy expected.

This forced me to separate two ideas that I had initially treated as the same thing:

Observation

Information describing the current state of the environment.

Action validity

Rules determining which actions the agent is allowed to take.

Those are related, but they are not necessarily the same object.

Moving the Constraint Out of the Observation

I removed action_mask from the Gymnasium observation dictionary.

The observation space needed to remain clean and consistent for Stable-Baselines3.

Instead, action validity was handled through the agent and environment logic.

The key rule became:

While active candidates N > 1:

    Do not allow direct candidate guesses.

    Select from valid question actions.

Once the deduction process is complete:

    Allow the final guess.

This was effectively a structural guardrail around exploration.

I was no longer asking the DQN to independently discover, from thousands of terrible possibilities, that guessing early was usually a bad idea.

The environment constrained the decision process so that the agent could actually experience the behaviour I wanted it to learn.

This raised an interesting question for me.

Was I making the problem easier in an artificial way?

In one sense, yes.

I was removing a major decision from the agent during most of the episode.

But I also started seeing it differently.

The goal of this experiment was not to see whether DQN could randomly discover that 4,200 possible guesses are usually worse than asking a question.

The interesting learning problem was:

Given the current search space, which question should the agent ask next?

The direct guessing decision had overwhelmed the actual experiment.

Constraining it allowed me to focus the learning problem on sequential information gathering.

I had fixed the most obvious issue with the action space.

But that did not mean the RL agent now understood the game.

I still had to decide what the agent should actually see.

The Next Problem Was the Observation

At this point, I had spent a lot of time thinking about actions:

What should the agent be allowed to do?

The next stage forced me to think about the other side of the problem:

What information does the agent need in order to make those decisions?

I wanted the agent to learn a strategy from interaction.

But there was a difficult balance.

If I gave it too little information, it might not understand the current search space well enough to make an informed decision.

If I gave it too much information, I risked exposing the underlying data so directly that the agent could effectively calculate the answer rather than learn a useful policy.

The next experiment became an attempt to find a representation between those two extremes.

Too little information
        │
        ▼
Agent cannot adapt to the current state


        VS.


Too much information
        │
        ▼
Agent effectively receives the solution

Finding the middle ground would become one of the most important design problems in the entire project.

What This Failure Taught Me

My first DQN experiment did not fail because the code simply had a bug.

It exposed several deeper problems in how I had framed the environment.

1. A valid action space can still be a bad learning space

Just because every action is logically possible does not mean every action should be equally available during exploration.

2. Action frequency matters

When thousands of bad actions dominate a handful of useful ones, unguided exploration can fail before meaningful learning begins.

3. RL environment design is part of the algorithm

The model does not learn in isolation.

The:

action space;
observation space;
reward;
transition rules;
termination conditions;

all shape what the agent is capable of learning.

4. Failure changed the direction of the project

Before this experiment, I was mainly asking:

Can I put DQN inside this game?

After it failed, the more interesting question became:

How do I represent this game so that learning the decision-making process is actually possible?
